WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.006
- All right, y'all,

00:00:01.006 --> 00:00:04.020
all hour we have celebrated
gutsy women in breaking the mold

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.540
and proving us ladies
really get things done.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.890
So, let's close things out

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.660
with one more trailblazing woman,

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:10.860
this is what I'm liking.

00:00:12.673 --> 00:00:14.220
(audience cheering)

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.920
When most of us hear a storm is coming,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.280
we get indoors and hunker
down to stay safe and dry,

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.290
because that's normal.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.600
That's not the case for everyone.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.880
Just check out the
photos one woman captures

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.320
when the clouds roll in.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.740
She hunts down tornadoes.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:32.730
Like the movie Twister,

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:34.470
except it's not a movie, it's real life.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.353
She gets too close for my
personal comfort to lightning.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.050
It's what?

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.990
She fearlessly faces the
elements, it's amazing,

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.370
that the photos are so beautiful.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:45.630
She's all about storm chasing.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.580
It's super crazy, but really cool.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.670
But Jen, from Colorado, isn't
just focused on the chase,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.350
she's also encouraging other women

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.540
to get into weather watching, meteorology,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.600
and science with her initiative
called Girls Who Chase.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.480
And we have Jen with us right now.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.790
Jen, welcome to the show.

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:02.623
(audience cheering)

00:01:02.623 --> 00:01:04.630
- Hi Kelly, thank you so much.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.210
- You're kinda crazy, but I love it.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.130
I love your kinda crazy, but
how did you get into this?

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.367
Or one day when you were
a kid you're just like,

00:01:09.367 --> 00:01:11.250
"Let's go find out what happens there."

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.270
Like what?

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.950
- Yeah, no, that's a great question.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.050
I mean, when I was young,

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.900
I was always into severe weather.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.990
And I watched the Weather
Channel all the time,

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.360
mostly instead of cartoons in fact.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.193
- Yeah.

00:01:25.193 --> 00:01:27.150
- And I really didn't get into it much,

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.760
until I was almost 40.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.520
In 2018, I went on a storm chasing tour

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.230
and was immediately hooked.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.810
And shortly after that in fact,

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.160
right near my house,

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.020
saw a storm come out of the foothills

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.160
just north of Denver.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.170
And I was in such a rush
to get out of my house,

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.060
I was still in my pajamas.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.350
Went chasing after it

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.630
and came on a hill

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.643
and saw my first tornado.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.190
(audience laughing)

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:57.000
- Like, did you hold metal up
like on the side on a hill?

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:57.833
It just sounds so crazy.

00:01:57.833 --> 00:02:00.000
But I mean, I think it's so exciting,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:00.930
I actually love storms.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.210
My kids hate when it
rains because I go nuts.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.470
I love rain, I love storms.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:05.580
I think they're beautiful.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.650
And they sound remarkable.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.170
But why did you start this
initiative, Girls Who Chase?

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:11.310
Why was this important to you?

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.150
I know you loved it,

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.170
but why was it so important to you

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.280
to introduce it to younger girls?

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.020
- Yeah, you know, storm chasing

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.500
is such a badass way to
illustrate women doing science.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:20.333
- Yeah.

00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:22.170
And, you know, when I
first started chasing,

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.700
I made a bunch of chase friends.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.710
And a lot of them were men

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.980
because storm chasing is
a male dominated area.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.720
And I noticed, in fact,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.100
that even though at times

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.230
I was the one calling the shots,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.960
and making the decisions
about where we were going,

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.870
and getting us to the right storm,

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.610
they would get a lot of the credit for it.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.470
So they would get patted on the back.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:44.303
- So weird.

00:02:44.303 --> 00:02:45.136
- [Jen] Yeah.

00:02:45.136 --> 00:02:46.530
Right, go figure.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.325
- Never happens in my industry either.

00:02:48.325 --> 00:02:49.158
(audience laughing)

00:02:49.158 --> 00:02:50.430
It's crazy, yeah.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.440
- Yeah, I know a lot of parallels there.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:53.673
- Yeah, no none at all.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.640
But it's exciting though

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.170
because I know you find this silly,

00:02:58.170 --> 00:02:59.190
but for nerds like us

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:00.930
that, like, we always revert to movies,

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.030
you're literally Helen Hunt in Twister

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.320
to me, in my brain movie.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.060
And I think it's incredible what you do.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.260
And I do think it's incredible

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.190
the knowledge you can find out

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.470
through those storms and
how you can save people.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.600
You know, with all
these destructive things

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.740
that happen with the environment

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:14.880
that really affect people.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.230
It's cool that we learn from them.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:17.940
And then we use that knowledge to help us

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.040
and prevent, you know, some catastrophes.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.630
So thank you so much for doing this.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:22.590
- Absolutely, thank you.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.027
- It's really cool.

00:03:24.027 --> 00:03:26.700
I'm liking Jen's post right now,

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:27.840
because it's awesome.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:28.890
And that's not all,

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.350
our season long partner
Pilot Pen Makers of Frixion,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.750
the go to erasable pen of
the Kelly Clarkson Show

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.610
want to celebrate those
creating fearlessly

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:36.780
just like you ma'am.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.070
So they're gonna give
you a thousand dollars.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.000
Just go have fun chasing.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:39.833
- Thank you.

